+# Investigation: relative-expression tests failing
+
+## Symptom
+
+- `Test - Strict mode` (`test/en/en_time_units_within.test.ts`) fails in the suite but passes alone: "in half a quarter" should give 2012-09-24 12:14 and gives 2012-11-24.
+- `Test - From now Expression` (`test/en/en_time_units_later.test.ts`) fails even alone: "half An hour from now" gives 13:14 instead of 12:44, right after "half an hour from now" worked.
+
+The result depends on what was parsed earlier in the same process, so there is shared state.
+
+## Root cause
+
+`parseDuration` in `src/locales/en/constants.ts` gained a cache (`parsedDurationCache`, key = lowercased text) that returns **the same `Duration` object** on every call.
+
+That object is passed to `addDuration` (`src/calculation/duration.ts`) via `ParsingComponents.createRelativeFromReference`, and `addDuration` **mutates** the object it receives: for fractional values it adds the remainder to the smaller unit (`{quarter: 0.5}` gains `month: 1.5`, `{hour: 0.5}` gains `minute: 30`). On the second parse of the same text, the cached object already carries those added keys and the remainder is added again: half a quarter becomes 1.5 + 1.5 months, half an hour becomes 30 + 30 minutes.
+
+That is why only fractional expressions parsed more than once in the same test file fail (jest isolates modules per file). "Strict mode" passes alone because, in isolation, it is the first time "in half a quarter" is parsed.
+
+## Fix
+
+The cache now stores a copy and returns a copy (`{ ...cached }`), so no caller receives the shared instance.
+
+I did not fix it by making `addDuration` immutable: `createRelativeFromReference` depends on the keys `addDuration` adds (e.g. `"hour" in duration` for "in half a day"), and changing that alters the result of other tests (`Test - The normal within expression`).
+
+## Verification
+
+The whole suite (`npx jest`) passes, including the two tests, with no change to the tests.
